# 6. INCIDENT PRIORITY AND STATUS

These are separate concepts and must remain separate throughout the application.

## 6.1 Priority

Priority describes the seriousness of the medical emergency.

Use:

- Critical
- Urgent
- Standard
- Unassessed

AI may suggest a priority based on clinically reviewed rules and the information collected.

The Operations Staff member must confirm or adjust it.

Insufficient information must not automatically produce Standard priority.

Use Unassessed and flag the incident for operator attention when the information is insufficient.

AI must never silently downgrade priority or make independent clinical decisions.

A qualified medical professional must review the clinical triage rules before live operational use.

## 6.2 Incident status

Use exactly five top-level incident statuses:

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| New | Incoming report, AI intake and initial information/location assessment |
| Under Review | Human takeover, assessment, verification, unit selection and dispatch approval |
| Dispatched | Dispatch confirmed, unit responding and arrival |
| Patient Transport | Patient picked up and being transported to a hospital |
| Closed | Response concluded and incident closed |

Detailed activities and timestamps belong in the incident timeline, not in additional top-level statuses.

For example, do not create separate statuses for:
- Call in progress.
- AI processing.
- Location pending.
- Awaiting operator.
- Awaiting dispatch approval.
- Unit en route.
- Unit arrived.
- Patient picked up.
- Hospital reached.

These are workflow activities or timeline events.

